,title,link,text,language,family
0,Kutenai language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kutenai_language,"March 23, 2026 - Kutenai (/ˈkuːtniː/ KOOT-nee), also Kootenai, Kootenay, Ktunaxa, and Ksanka, is the native language of the Kutenai people of Montana and Idaho in the United States and British Columbia in Canada. It is typically considered a language isolate, unrelated to the Salishan family of languages ...",Kutenai,Language isolate
1,Kutenai - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kutenai,"2 weeks ago - The Kutenai (/ˈkuːtəneɪ, -niː/ ... Kutenai bands live in southeastern British Columbia, northern Idaho, and western Montana. The Kutenai language is a language isolate, thus unrelated to the languages of neighboring peoples or any other known language....",Kutenai,Language isolate
2,"Kutenai alphabet, pronunciation and language",https://www.omniglot.com/writing/kutenai.htm,"Other books documenting Kutenai have been published since then, including a collection of stories, The Kutenai Tales, by Franz Boas in 1918. Since 2012 the Ktunaxa people in Canada have been working to revitalize their language. They have collected audio recordings of words, phrases, stories and songs, which are shared online and in an app.",Kutenai,Language isolate
3,Language Isolates - First Nations Languages of British Columbia - Research Guides at University of British Columbia,https://guides.library.ubc.ca/Languages_of_BC/Language_Isolates,"February 19, 2025 - OLAC resources in and about the Kutenai language (bibliography and some online text)",Kutenai,Language isolate
4,Ktunaxa/Ksanka Language,https://www.languagegeek.com/isolate/ktunaxa.html,"The Canadian Census counts 200 Kutenai speakers in 2006, down from from 220 in 2001. ... This is a list of the Ktunaxa/Ksanka speaking communities. In parentheses are the English place names followed by the number of speakers in that community according to the 2006 Canadian Census. Because of the small numbers of speakers, the census results are likely overestimates. ... Loanwords may contain ‹č› for the English /ch/ sound. If you are having problems viewing the language ...",Kutenai,Language isolate
5,"Kutenai | Indigenous, Plateau, Language | Britannica",https://www.britannica.com/topic/Kutenai,"July 20, 1998 - Kutenai, North American Indian tribe that traditionally lived in what are now southeastern British Columbia, Can., and northern Idaho and northwestern Montana in the United States. Their language, also called Kutenai, is probably best considered a language isolate; that is, it is unrelated to other",Kutenai,Language isolate
6,Kutenai language facts for kids,https://kids.kiddle.co/Kutenai_language,"October 17, 2025 - This is called language revitalization. Tribal councils from different Ktunaxa communities are helping. They have put audio recordings of Kutenai words and phrases online. You can find these on the FirstVoices website.",Kutenai,Language isolate
7,Kutenai language,https://ipfs.io/ipfs/QmXoypizjW3WknFiJnKLwHCnL72vedxjQkDDP1mXWo6uco/wiki/Kutenai_language.html,Kutenai is a language isolate.,Kutenai,Language isolate
8,"Kootenay Language and the Kootenai Indian Tribe (Kutenai, Ktunaxa)",http://www.native-languages.org/kootenai.htm,Ktunaxa (Kutenai) Language Online Kootenai pronunciation guide and sample text. First Peoples Language Map of BC Interactive map of Kootenay and other indigenous language communities in British Columbia. Kutenai Language Kutenai Phonology Kutenai Grammar Kutenai Indian Text: Scanned-in Kootenay ...,Kutenai,Language isolate
9,About: Kutenai language,https://dbpedia.org/page/Kutenai_language,"The Kutenai language (/ˈkuːtəneɪ, -i/), also Kootenai, Kootenay, Ktunaxa, and Ksanka, is the native language of the Kutenai people of Montana and Idaho in the United States and British Columbia in Canada. It is typically considered a language isolate, unrelated to the Salishan family of languages spoken by neighboring tribes on the coast and in the interior Plateau.",Kutenai,Language isolate
